Translation patch: `ComChangeUser`, `ComChangeUserMoreData`, `HandshakeResponse` (connect attributes and combinators)

Change:
  - The connect-attributes map `HashMap<RawBytes<LenEnc>, RawBytes<LenEnc>>` is named `misc::trusted::ConnectAttrs` (a type alias), and every `HashMap` operation goes through a new function of the module of patch 64: `connect_attrs_new` (`HashMap::new()`, also replacing `&Default::default()`), `connect_attrs_insert`, `connect_attrs_entries` (the `(key, value)` byte slices in iteration order), `connect_attrs_from_strings` (the `into_iter().map(..).collect()` from `HashMap<String, String>`), `connect_attrs_into_owned`, `connect_attrs_to_strings` (the getter's `iter().map(..as_str().into_owned()..).collect()`).
  - `serialize_connect_attrs` takes the entries once and computes the length (`connect_attrs_len`) and writes them (`serialize_connect_attrs_entries`, each key and value with `<LenEnc as BytesRepr>::serialize`) by recursion over the entries; `MySerialize for HandshakeResponse` calls it instead of its own copy of that code. `deserialize_connect_attrs`'s loop becomes the recursive `deserialize_connect_attrs_entries`.
  - Combinators become `match`es: `opt.map(RawBytes::new).unwrap_or_default()`, `opt.map(|x| x.into_owned())`, `buf.parse(()).map(Some)?`, `opt.as_ref().map(..)`, `opt.as_ref()`, `scramble_buf.map(Into::into).unwrap_or_default()` (hoisted before the `if`, so the conversion is done once), and `server_version >= (5, 5, 3)` is written out lexicographically.
  - Optional fields: the parsing of each optional field (`more_data`, the change-user `auth_plugin`, `connect_attributes`, `scramble_buf`, `db_name`, the response `auth_plugin`) moves into a helper taking `&mut ParseBuf` and returning the field; the optional trailing fields of the two serializers are written by helpers in tail position; `HandshakeResponse::new`'s `scramble_buf` and `connect_attributes` are built by helpers (`new_scramble_buf`, `opt_connect_attrs_from_strings`).
Why: `HashMap` (std, with a random hasher) cannot be translated: the module is `opaque`, and each function has an opaque Lean model. Aeneas fails on closures and constructors as values, does not model `Option::as_ref`/`unwrap_or_default`, tuple `PartialOrd` or the `HashMap` iterators, fails on loops over slices whose elements hold borrows, on joins after an `if` that advances a `ParseBuf` or borrows `self` (patch 30), and ("Internal error") on the `HashMap<String, String>` conversion and the `if`/`else if` building `scramble_buf` inside `HandshakeResponse::new`.
Behaviour: Identical. Each `match` is the definition of the combinator; `(a, b, c) >= (5, 5, 3)` is the lexicographic comparison written out; the helpers parse and write the same fields in the same order with the same early `Err` returns. The two iterations of `serialize_connect_attrs` (length, then entries) see the map's entries in its iteration order, which does not change between them, so taking the entries once gives the same bytes; the length is summed with the same overflow behaviour. `RawBytes<LenEnc>::serialize` is `LenEnc::serialize(self.0.as_ref(), buf)`, which equals the `as_bytes()` form used by the entries because `LenEnc::MAX_LEN = usize::MAX` (`as_bytes` does not truncate). For `HandshakeResponse`, `connect_attributes` is parsed via `CapabilityFlags::from_bits_retain(raw).contains(CLIENT_CONNECT_ATTRS)`, which equals `raw & CLIENT_CONNECT_ATTRS.bits() > 0` for this one-bit flag. The recursion uses stack proportional to the number of attributes.

